# ============================================================
# FILE: .gitignore
# CONSTRUCTION ORDER: #1 — The very first file created
# HOW: Typed by hand in the project root before the first `git init` or `git commit`
# WHY NOW: Must exist before any commit so that secrets, build outputs,
#          and generated files are NEVER accidentally tracked in version control.
#          Once a secret is committed, it lives in git history forever
#          even if you delete it later. This file prevents that.
# ============================================================

# ── These two lines are project-specific exclusions ──────────────────────────
# The old Case Study 1 folder and the requirements PDF are intentionally
# excluded so the graded submission only contains the new project code.
case-study-1-reference/
CASE_STUDY_2_REQUIREMENTS.pdf

# ── node_modules/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# npm install regenerates this folder from package.json.
# Committing it would add tens of thousands of files to git history —
# making clones extremely slow and diff output unreadable.
node_modules/
backend/node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/

# ── Build outputs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `npm run build` compiles TypeScript → JavaScript into dist/.
# These are generated files — they should never be committed because:
#   1. They bloat the repo
#   2. They must always be rebuilt from source to ensure they are up to date
#   3. CI/CD builds them fresh on every pipeline run
backend/dist/
backend/dist-seed/   # output folder for the seed script's own tsconfig
frontend/dist/

# ── Environment secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env holds DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, and other sensitive values.
# If this were committed, anyone with repo access (or who ever clones it)
# would have the production database password and JWT signing key.
# NEVER commit .env — use .env.example (which IS committed) for documentation.
backend/.env
backend/.env.local

# ── Test coverage reports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `npm run test:coverage` generates HTML/XML reports in coverage/.
# These are artifacts, not source — they are uploaded as CI artifacts instead.
backend/coverage/

# ── macOS system files ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .DS_Store is created automatically by macOS Finder in every directory.
# It stores folder view preferences and should never go into the repo.
.DS_Store

# ── IDE / editor settings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each developer's editor config is personal. Committing it would override
# another developer's preferences. Shared settings belong in .editorconfig instead.
.vscode/
.idea/

# ── Log files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Log files grow unboundedly and are environment-specific.
# They are never meaningful as source code.
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# ── Terraform state and secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# .terraform/ contains downloaded provider plugins (~100MB) — regenerated by `terraform init`
# terraform.tfvars contains sensitive variable values (db_password, jwt_secret)
# *.tfstate files contain the live state of real AWS infrastructure —
#   they include resource IDs, ARNs, and potentially secrets in plaintext.
#   In a team setting, state belongs in an S3 backend (see provider.tf), not git.
infra/terraform/.terraform/
infra/terraform/terraform.tfvars
infra/terraform/*.tfstate
infra/terraform/*.tfstate.backup
